<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kyiv-Mohyla Academy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1632">1632</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Kiev-Mohyla Academy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kyiv-Mohyla Academy was a school of higher learning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>, formed in 1632 when Metropolitan Petro Mohyla merged the Kyiv Brotherhood school with his monastery school. Teaching in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Church Slavonic on the model of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> colleges, it became the leading centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> education in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, training clergy and officials for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. It was closed in 1817 and revived as a university in 1991.</p></div>
